course: dictionary
lesson: 11231
title: The many meanings of word
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A word is a unit of language with a meaning, like «apple» or «run». Sentences are built from words, and each word does a job. When you meet a word you do not know, the words around it, its context, can help you find its meaning.
explain: A word can also be a short talk. «Can I have a word?» asks for a quick chat, not for a single spoken item. The sentence tells you which meaning is right, so always read the whole sentence before you decide.
explain: Your word is a promise. «I give you my word» means you will really do what you said. «Keep your word» means keep that promise. «Word for word» means copying exactly, with every single word the same.
fact: A word is a unit of language with a meaning.
fact: A word can be a short talk, as in «Can I have a word?»
fact: «I give you my word» means I promise.
fact: A word problem is a maths problem told in sentences.
fact: Cue words are clue words that point to the maths operation you need to use.
exercise: In «Can I have a word?», what does word mean? | A loud shout | *A short talk | A sum to solve | A large book
exercise: «I give you my word» means which? | A loud noise | A list of spellings | A question | *A promise
exercise: In maths, a «word problem» is which of these? | A problem with no numbers at all | A spelling test | *A maths problem told in sentences | A science experiment
test: «Keep your word» means which? | *Keep your promise | Go to the shop | Spell the word wrong | Lose the page
test: Which phrase means copying something exactly, every single word? | *Word for word | In other words | Cue word | Give your word
exam: Which option best describes a word? | A paragraph of writing | *A unit of language with a meaning, like «apple» | A bird's song | A loud shout
